## Summary

Update the Relay landing page using the review note, cursor/Flare targets, motion gesture, mobile demonstration, and hover/focus evidence in this synthetic design review.

- The replacement H1 appears only in the temporary review note shown in the recording.
- Five requests use deictic visual or cursor references; the mobile overflow request is fully spoken.
- The requested primary CTA is intentionally counter-conventional and must be grounded from the Flare evidence.

## Intent

- Type: feedback review
- Objective: Update the Relay landing page using the review note, cursor/Flare targets, motion gesture, mobile demonstration, and hover/focus evidence in this synthetic design review.

## Requested changes

- Change the hero H1 to ‘Move work forward without another meeting.’
- Make ‘See how it works’ the sole filled primary hero CTA and render ‘Start free’ as the outlined secondary action.
- Hide only the ‘Resources’ navigation link below 640 CSS pixels while keeping the other navigation links.
- Move ‘Cancel anytime’ immediately before ‘No credit card’ and preserve all three assurance items.
- At 390 CSS pixels, keep all three proof cards while removing page-wide horizontal overflow.
- On ‘See how it works,’ keep white text on hover and keyboard focus and add a visible focus ring with at least 3:1 contrast.

## Draft implementation plan

_Drafted deterministically from the extracted requests. Validate each step against the linked evidence before changing anything._

1. Confirm the requested scope against the nearest key-moment frames, transcript timestamps, and browser diagnostics; note capture gaps before editing.
2. Locate the owning code paths and implement the requested changes in dependency order while preserving unrelated behavior.
3. Exercise every requested outcome in the built product, compare it with the recording evidence, and report anything that could not be confirmed.

## Key moments

Timestamped moments where the speaker points at something on screen. Each frame image shows exactly what was visible; coordinates (when present) are the pointer position as fractions of frame width/height, sampled at the same instant as the frame, and frames with coordinates have a red-and-white marker burned in at that spot. Read "source" to know what the pointer was doing: "click"/"fused" mean a real recorded click, "hover" means the cursor was resting there without clicking.

Cursor telemetry: PRESENT - coordinates below come from the recorded cursor track. A moment with no coordinates means the cursor was in transit at that instant, so no honest pointer position exists for it.

